   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Digital Rights and Inclusion Forum</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">DRIF23, the Digital Rights and Inclusion Forum held from 12th to 14th April 2023 with 348 physical attendees and 253 virtual attendees in Nairobi, Kenya, encompassed a wide array of discussions and sessions aimed at advancing digital rights and inclusion in Africa. The event featured dialogues on topics such as digital safety for vulnerable communities, online gender-based violence, challenges in big tech and content moderation, the digital gender gap, combating misinformation, and empowering female journalists against online abuse, among others.

One notable highlight was the closed Digital Security Training (DST) tailored for journalists, human rights defenders, and civil society actors facing digital security threats. The DST was part of the larger Digital Security Clinic held during DRIF23, accompanied by a Digital Security Help Desk. Additionally, panel discussions delved into issues such as data privacy, accessibility for persons with disabilities, the impact of Internet Freedom projects, government responses to disinformation, fair data practices, human rights in technology, and the role of ICT in post-COVID-19 education. DRIF23 also hosted a training workshop in collaboration with the African Parliament Network on Internet Governance (APNIG), focusing on the future of digital rights in Africa for Members of Parliament, policymakers, and civil society representatives. Furthermore, a funders&#x2019; panel organized by PIN brought together prominent funders to shed light on funding opportunities in the digital rights and inclusion sector.</narrative>
   </description>
